# Cursor's CLI agent as a kiln harness in a Shards microVM (kiln plan
# 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 27; plan 01a1ce705e5c7504b88732f443d99288, research
# 01a1ce705e5c7504bdcac9721fa699e3). kiln runs it as its ACP server, `agent acp` (Kind "cursor",
# crates/kiln/src/harness/kinds.rs). It comes in as pin.json pins it, in ./cursor-<arch>/: Cursor's
# own Linux package, whose SHA-256 equals the .sha256 Cursor publishes beside it (Cursor signs
# nothing; pin.sh), unpacked with its top directory stripped as Cursor's installer does, and
# harness.json. Nothing else installs it, and no credential is in the image.

# Cursor's package is a bash launcher and a bundled Node whose binaries link glibc (at most
# GLIBC_2.28 and GLIBCXX_3.4.22, and libz), and Cursor publishes no musl build: the base needs glibc
# and bash, and the image has no variant. This default, step 19's, pinned by digest, has both and runs
# the package with no RUN; Alpine can't start it.
ARG BASE=buildpack-deps:trixie-scm@sha256:4f34e1d673fdb65371de9f88bdd32c4e30152fe692b35604aef5c0b3c0e6da10
FROM ${BASE}

# BuildKit sets TARGETARCH in the global scope, so the stage declares it to name each platform's
# harness directory.
ARG TARGETARCH
HARNESS cursor FROM ./cursor-${TARGETARCH}

# Its network, every part named (default deny; Shards D236, destinations by name): a network for this
# harness alone, which it joins with no flow to any member, and TCP out to the hosts below alone, by
# name, through the network's boundary (--egress) and the microVM's (EXPOSE ... AS egress). Each name
# resolves for it through the agents' resolver, and no other does. Nothing comes in; no other host,
# port or protocol goes out (kiln plan 01a1cd8e862e7169a074db3d5fa33f3d, step 18:
# docs/research/s18-host-in-vm says where each host comes from).
# - api2.cursor.sh (most of its requests, and its sign-in's poll) and api2direct.cursor.sh (its
#   direct route to the same service), both named by the CLI's own code; api5.cursor.sh and its six
#   agent hosts, an agent's requests; repo42.cursor.sh, codebase search (cursor.com/docs/enterprise/
#   network-configuration). Cursor Tab's hosts (the editor's), its sign-in pages (the person's
#   browser), and updates and extensions (off: --disable-auto-update) go nowhere.
# kiln's MCP isn't granted here, nor in any of kiln's Agentfiles: it reaches the harness over the link
# kiln drives it by, as that link's own `mcp` lane (Shards D254), never over the network.
NETWORK --egress=agent.api5.cursor.sh:443 --egress=agent.global.api5.cursor.sh:443 --egress=agent.us.api5.cursor.sh:443 --egress=agentn.api5.cursor.sh:443 --egress=agentn.global.api5.cursor.sh:443 --egress=agentn.us.api5.cursor.sh:443 --egress=api2.cursor.sh:443 --egress=api2direct.cursor.sh:443 --egress=api5.cursor.sh:443 --egress=repo42.cursor.sh:443 provider FOR cursor
EXPOSE --harnesses=cursor 443/tcp AS egress FOR provider
CONNECT cursor WITH cursor ON provider

# Its project is plugged in as it starts, at the path it has on the machine (Shards D235), so kiln
# reads its diffs, HEAD and checkpoints at that same path, and no volume holds it. Its HOME is the
# person's volume, whole: harness.json sets HOME=/home/cursor, kiln's one rule for every harness it
# ships (step 18). A harness sees its image read-only, with its own /tmp scratch, so a HOME that is
# writable and kept from run to run is a volume, and what the programs it runs keep in a home (a
# .gitconfig, a tool's sign-in) is kept with it. Cursor keeps its config, sessions, MCP servers,
# rules, plugins and hooks in $HOME/.cursor, and on Linux its sign-in in $HOME/.config/cursor.
# HOME in run.env needs a shards at or after 997f4e47e (v0.8.0 and later), where run.env replaces
# init's HOME=/tmp. The volume is the harness's alone, and outside /tmp, which the scratch covers.
VOLUME --target-kind=harness /home/cursor FOR cursor
